---
import MachineryFamily from "../../../components/studies/MachineryFamily.astro";
import SolidUtilityFamily from "../../../components/studies/SolidUtilityFamily.astro";
import StorageVolumes from "../../../components/studies/StorageVolumes.astro";
import { studies } from "../../../data/studies";
import StudyLayout from "../../../layouts/StudyLayout.astro";

const study = studies.find((s) => s.slug === "open-frame-machinery-2026-11-02");
---

<StudyLayout study={study}>
  <aside aria-label="Study closure">
    <h2>Closed / Exploration stopped</h2>
    <p>
      Closed on 2 November 2026 by designer instruction. No utility design was
      accepted. The designer identified 02.2 as the last useful visual step and
      described this as the most successful of the three runs, while judging the
      later exploration to have entered a loop.
    </p>
    <p>
      The positive finding was the importance of substantial objects belonging
      to the game world. A fresh-cycle prompt and a proposed reference panel of
      all four ships and engines were discussed before closure. Engines in that
      proposed panel face exhaust-down. These are recorded discussion outcomes;
      their effect on a future study remains untested.
    </p>
    <p>
      Evidence record:
      <code>docs/loop-evidence/2026-11-02-open-frame-machinery-closure.md</code
      >.
    </p>
  </aside>
  <section>
    <h2>01 / Machinery with a visible job</h2>
    <p>
      A utility mounted beside an engine needs its own character without
      competing with the ship. At flight scale, a seam or indicator disappears:
      the working mass and the gaps around it must identify the machine.
      Enlarged, those same shapes should resolve into deliberately assembled
      solid parts.
    </p>
    <p>
      This exploration asks whether exposed mechanisms held in a common
      structural frame can unite storage, solar charging, gravity charging and
      repair. The established ships and engines supply the material discipline:
      top-down projection, flat facets, light from above-left and dark recesses.
      The proposed distinction is construction around a visible working part,
      rather than a miniature hull.
    </p>
    <p>
      The decision here is the family’s visual construction and the four
      distinguishing silhouettes. Charging rules, repair balance and animation
      timing remain outside this visual round.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2>02 / Exposed mechanisms</h2>
    <details>
      <summary>
        02.1 / One frame, four working parts — flat construction ruled out
      </summary>
      <p>
        Review finding: this construction does not yet belong to the game’s
        aesthetic. The Wasp hull and ion engine read as faux-3D objects; these
        utilities read as flat vector icons. Exposed components alone do not
        establish volume. Rusty or assembled-component styling also does not
        provide the desired direction. The sketches remain below as evidence of
        this unsuccessful visual approach, not an accepted family.
      </p>
      <p>
        The settled requirement is solid machinery seen from above. Each part
        needs a physical volume, with visible planes derived from its real
        edges, height and connection to other parts. The family’s eventual
        character is still open; icon-like construction is unsuitable regardless
        of that character. A subsequent comparison should test this volumetric
        construction before expanding the visual vocabulary.
      </p>
      <p>
        Current production hull and engine polygons anchor the comparison. Below
        them are procedural vector sketches, all viewed directly from above.
        Shared rails and clamps hold different working masses; muted metal and
        restrained cool surfaces keep the machinery subordinate to the ship.
      </p>
      <MachineryFamily />
      <p>
        The enlarged drawings reveal the flat construction identified in review;
        the 0.65× samples expose lost detail. These are static concept fixtures
        at provisional dimensions, not installed equipment or evidence of
        functional mechanics. The gravity rotor is a visual hypothesis, not a
        claim about how gravity charging works.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>
        02.2 / Build a solid object first — solid construction retained
      </summary>
      <p>
        Human review established that these proposals communicate a solid object
        existing in the game world more successfully than the previous sketches.
        This validates the construction direction, not any particular shape or
        format. Open mesh and exposed parts must not dictate the object before
        its mass is established.
      </p>
      <p>
        The first comparison established a useful boundary: exposed parts
        arranged like icons do not share the hull and engine’s faux-3D
        construction. That negative result narrows the inquiry without settling
        the family’s character.
      </p>
      <p>
        Energy storage provides one simple role for comparing three
        constructions. Each proposal uses a low cradle, a cool metal palette and
        light from above-left. Broad planes describe raised solid parts; dark
        gaps separate those parts. The shapes are top-down projections, not an
        angled camera view.
      </p>
      <StorageVolumes />
      <p>
        Look first for believable mass and depth beside the established ion
        engine above, then for what survives in the small samples. These static
        sketches test construction only: no capacity, fit, animation or final
        material choice is implied. A successful volume can inform the other
        three utilities; another unsuccessful comparison is useful if it
        identifies which construction still reads flat.
      </p>
    </details>

    <h3>02.3 / Four jobs, one solid construction</h3>
    <p>
      02.2 established a useful direction: substantial parts can read as objects
      in the game world. No particular format was accepted. This comparison
      carries that direction across the four requested roles, testing whether
      shared construction can support distinct machinery silhouettes.
    </p>
    <p>
      The family hypothesis is compact industrial machinery built around its
      working mass. Storage repeats two cells; solar surrounds a converter with
      thin collector wings; gravity places opposing weights around an axle;
      repair puts articulated jaws around a service core. Cool metal, broad
      upper planes and darker shoulders provide a common construction language.
      Exposed parts arise from each role rather than from a common decorative
      opening.
    </p>
    <SolidUtilityFamily />
    <p>
      Compare the four as a family beside the ion engine, then inspect whether
      each silhouette survives at small scale. These are provisional top-down
      concept drawings, not fitted equipment. In particular, the gravity
      mechanism and repair jaws are visual hypotheses; their operating
      principles, motion and gameplay remain undecided. Shared palette alone is
      insufficient: the comparison succeeds only if the objects appear related
      through their construction and still have distinct jobs.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section><h2>03 / To be developed after 02</h2></section>
</StudyLayout>
